      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "HISTORY AND CLINICAL FINDINGS",
          "content": "A 49-year-old woman presented with increased bone and muscle pain of the left thigh and also of the left ribs since 1 1/2 year. 2 osteolytic regions on the left proximal femur were punctured but no tumour-like changes were found."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INVESTIGATIONS",
          "content": "Laboratory tests showed a severe hypophosphataemia (0.33 mmol/l), increased serum alkaline phosphatase activity and moderate elevated parathyroid hormone. Renal loss of phosphate was measured under phosphate substitution. The bone mineral density (DXA) was decreased. With octreotid scintigraphy a somatostatine receptor positive tumour was detected on the right proximal thigh and magnetic resonance scanning confirmed localization of the tumour."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "TREATMENT AND COURSE",
          "content": "Treatment with oral phosphate and calcitriol improved the complaints presently. After total removal of a mesenchymal tumour, normalization of serum phosphate occurred and the patient did not require any medicine and did not have complaints anymore."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "In a case of uncertain hypophosphataemic osteomalacia in adults it is essential to search for a tumour after exclusion of the rare differential diagnoses to enable a causal treatment of a potentially oncogenic osteomalacia."
        }
      ]},
